MappedParameter = {
    ("EngagementCodeList", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[EngagementCodeList] ), 0),
    ("AlphaCode", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[AlphaCode] ), 1),
    ("CustodianDisplayName", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[CustodianDisplayName] ), 2),
    ("EFileName", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[EFileName] ), 3)
}
// Lets a report viewer swap which column drives the aster plot's Category
// role via a slicer, without touching the spec.
//
// CONFIRMED IN POWER BI DESKTOP: a field parameter's label/value column
// CANNOT be overridden per-visual the way an ordinary column or measure can
// via "Rename for this visual". The table is called "MappedParameter", and
// its own label column (Fields pane: MappedParameter table -> Category /
// MappedParameter Fields / MappedParameter Order) happens to be named
// "Category" -- but that is NOT the field name Deneb actually receives
// data under. Confirmed live (Deneb's "view as table" and a plain Power BI
// table visual both showed the same thing): when Power BI resolves a
// field-parameter-driven role, the data is keyed by the REAL underlying
// column's own name -- whichever of EngagementCodeList/AlphaCode/
// CustodianDisplayName/EFileName is currently selected -- not by this
// table's label column at all. The spec's field-mapping adapter
// (visuals/aster-plot/spec/aster-plot.vl.json, first transform step)
// handles this by checking each of the 4 candidates in order via
// isValid() and using whichever one actually has data -- keep that in
// sync with the candidate list here. See the README's "Field mapping"
// section for the full writeup and what to touch for a second,
// independent visual's own field parameter.
//
// If you ever reference this table's columns (Category / MappedParameter
// Fields / MappedParameter Order) in a measure, use MAX, not SELECTEDVALUE
// or ALLSELECTED -- Power BI links them into a composite key ("Group By
// Columns"), and touching any single one via a filter-context function
// throws "part of composite key" (confirmed live). See
// dax/measure-a-color-gradient.dax for the full writeup.
